      "note": "THE 1853 HOUSE (Andreas vol. 1, p. 181: Lockport gray marble, the main part 100 feet square, porticoes north and south with stone steps): a rusticated basement and three storeys after the storey added in 1870 (vol. 2, p. 66), round-headed windows below and square above, the porticoes carrying low pediments, a low roof; on it THE CUPOLA, a square base, then a round drum of tall arched openings with a balustraded gallery (the fire watch's), a second drum with the clock and a ribbed dome with a lantern and staff, as the stereograph shows it; and the smaller octagonal belfry with a bell-shaped cap and the small domed cupola beside it (vol. 1: 'two domes and a cupola'). THE WINGS of 1870 (Lemont stone, vol. 2, p. 66): a rusticated basement and three storeys of round-arched windows with moulded heads, a bracketed cornice and balustraded parapet, a projecting centre pavilion on the north and south fronts crowned by a scrolled crest, and low domes on the corners. Storey heights and the wings' bays are read off the stereograph against the 1853 house's 100-foot front (reconstructed where the pictures cannot say)."
    },
